RS-4 pre-work patch 02: server re-grading for Studio v2 engines (owner-truth patch 01 generalised to every v2 engine).
Applies after Wave 2 integration to: server/studio/grade.js (RS-4 owns server/studio/** after integration).

Why: the v2 host grades in the page for instant feedback, but evidence must come from the server. The server stores the
VALIDATED spec when it serves the piece (validateSpec output, never the planner's raw spec) and re-grades each raw act
with the same shared function. Answers that carry host-recorded input logs (phase-shift controls, food-web actions,
shadow-play samples) are bigger than W2-H's 512-byte bound: measured maxima are in REPORT.md (answerBytes column);
the v2 bound is 256 KB. Engines rate-limit their channels (phase-shift: an entry only when an input changes, at most
10/s; food-web: ≤ 1 action per species per 1.2 s; shadow-play: 10 Hz samples, ≤ 25 s per step). Worst case is
phase-shift dragged non-stop for 10 minutes (≈ 6,000 entries × ~45 B ≈ 270 KB): if that bound is ever hit, switch the
phase-shift grader to a per-step checkpoint the SERVER computes (it replays the previous step's log itself and keeps
the end state), so each answer carries only its own step's log. Not built in the pre-work: the pure gradeAnswer replays
the whole log, which is exact and simple.

Node strips TypeScript types by default from v22.18, and shared/studio-spec.ts is erasable-syntax only, so the server can
import it directly. Verified locally on v22.22.0 only. NOT verified in the image: both Dockerfiles use the floating
`node:22-slim` tag; before merging run `docker run --rm node:22-slim node -e "import('./x.ts')"` style check, or pin
`node:22.22-slim`. If the image is older, add `--experimental-strip-types` to the server start command.

--- a/server/studio/grade.js
+++ b/server/studio/grade.js
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 import { archetype } from "./archetypes/index.js";
 import { graderFor } from "./qa/graders.js";
 import { fromLegacyEvidence } from "../learner/kt/adapter.js";
+import { ARCHETYPES_V2, gradeAnswer, validateSpec } from "../../shared/studio-spec.ts";

 export const GRADER_VERSION = "studio-grade@1";
 /** Answers arrive from an untrusted frame: bound the JSON before it reaches a grader. */
 export const MAX_ANSWER_BYTES = 512;
+/** Studio v2 answers may carry the host's input record (bounded per engine; see REPORT.md answerBytes). */
+export const MAX_ANSWER_BYTES_V2 = 256 * 1024;
+
+/**
+ * A grading session for a Studio v2 piece. `spec` is what the server served (it validates again, so a stored spec
+ * that predates a schema change still grades against a valid one). Never throws; never reads a `correct` field.
+ * @param {string} archetypeId @param {unknown} spec
+ */
+export function createGradeSessionV2(archetypeId, spec) {
+  if (!ARCHETYPES_V2.includes(archetypeId)) throw new Error("not a v2 archetype: " + archetypeId);
+  const valid = validateSpec(archetypeId, spec).spec;
+  const wrongs = new Map();
+  return {
+    archetype: archetypeId,
+    /** @param {{ itemId: string, value: unknown }} act */
+    grade(act) {
+      let v = null, itemId = "";
+      try {
+        const s = JSON.stringify(act ?? null);
+        if (s && s.length <= MAX_ANSWER_BYTES_V2) { const o = JSON.parse(s); itemId = String(o?.itemId ?? "").slice(0, 64); v = o?.value ?? null; }
+      } catch { v = null; }
+      const g = gradeAnswer(archetypeId, valid, itemId, v);
+      const triesBefore = wrongs.get(itemId) ?? 0;
+      if (g.verdict !== "right" && g.verdict !== "ungraded") wrongs.set(itemId, triesBefore + 1);
+      return { correct: g.verdict === "right", verdict: g.verdict, itemId, truth: g.truth, detail: g.detail, triesBefore, closedItem: g.verdict === "right" };
+    },
+  };
+}

Evidence wiring (unchanged rule): an item's episode closes on the first host-correct grade; ONE kt_evidence event per
item episode via 'studio'. `detail` carries a kit misconception id when the act matches its signature (e.g.
c4-maths-ch04-t01-m-drop-zero, c6-maths-ch02-t03-m-wrong-scale, c7-maths-ch13-t02-m-mean-in-data); RS-5's
classifier may read it as a candidate misconception, never as a verdict.
